<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fort Príncipe da Beira</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1776">1776</a>–<a href="/years/1783">1783</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Real Forte Príncipe da Beira</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fort Príncipe da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beira">Beira</a> is a large stone fortress on the right bank of the Guaporé river, in the Brazilian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rondonia">Rondônia</a>, built by the Portuguese between 1776 and 1783. It guarded the western frontier of Mato Grosso against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and was one of the largest fortifications of colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. Remote and later abandoned, it survives largely intact.</p></div>
